;;; `stale-naf-fork.ein` with `(config :warn-derived-naf true)` — the twin that
;;; shows M1e2 **W8** is *not* contained by the S1e.2.3 diagnostic. Register
;;; row `W8/stale-naf-fork`, as a twin.
;;;
;;; As banked, below this header the program was `stale-naf-fork.ein` plus the
;;; one `(config …)` line. The walk's answer was the same — `Solution` k = 1,
;;; `{(p one)}` — and the run narrated **no `warn` event of either category**:
;;; the diagnostic named a rule that concludes `(false)` from an `(absent …)`
;;; (`naf-upward-closure.ein`'s shape), and here the `absent` is in a rule that
;;; concludes a positive, and the contradiction is reached by a different rule
;;; reading what it left behind.
;;;
;;; Banked from M1e2's probes at S1e2.1.1.
;;;
;;; ── 2026-11-04 — refused, *via* (M1e2 S1e2.2.5) ───────────────────────────
;;;
;;; As `stale-naf-fork.ein`: `p-and-q-clash` rests on `(absent (q ?x))`
;;; through `p`, which F2's *via* (ruled 2026-11-04) counts, so the program is
;;; refused at load. What this twin showed, that S1e.2.3's warning does not
;;; see the derived case, is why the refusal reads derivations and the warning
;;; did not. The same stage retired `:warn-derived-naf`, so the `(config …)`
;;; line went and the program below is `stale-naf-fork.ein`'s; the file stays as
;;; the register's twin, whose record says what the walk answered with the flag.
;;; Re-taken at M1e2 S1e2.2.6 (2026-11-04): refused at the same commands as
;;; `stale-naf-fork.ein`, with the same line, and `ein saturate` still runs.

(relation thing T :why "{?1} is a thing")
(relation p T :why "p holds of {?1}")
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?1}")
(thing one)
(rule derive-p ()
  :match  (and (thing ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (p ?x) :why "{?x} is a thing and no q fact holds of it, so p holds of {?x}")
(rule p-and-q-clash ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (q ?x))
  :assert (false) :why "both p and q hold of {?x}, which cannot be")
(query :goal (q ?x))
